A||||16||Formula||Future Mods
Z||000000||Formula||07-21-99||07:38 AM||||Here is the mods that I want to make to my 1988 bright red fiero formula(2.8L,Auto, black wheels, etc...).  It currently needs painted because I let someone else drive it and they hit a stop sign(CRY).<br>Here they go......<br>3.1 Liter V6 H.O. Balanced and blue printed<br>ADS SuperChip<br>K&N air and Oil filters<br>Cat Back exhaust (Stainless???)<br>High Performance Headers<br>.........................<br>Bright Red Met. Paint<br>yellow vinyl for FORMULA and FIERO stickers<br>shaved pontiac badge in back<br>tinted windows<br>.........................<br>Clarion C.D. Player<br>Boston Acoustics Tweeters, Mid-Ranges,woofers<br>Rockford fosgate punch 8" subwoofer underdash<br>Jensen 200 W amp<br>new or covered seats<br>new headliner<br>wood grain dash kit<br>GT steering wheel<br>Dash cover<br>new gauges<br>..............................<br>Respond and tell me what you think is good and bad.  I estimate this as being a 6 or 7 thousand dollar project so it will take a while after I go back to school in sept.<br>Thanks for responding
Z||000001||lowCG||07-21-99||12:00 PM||||Sounds like some pretty sensible upgrades,would make sure the chip you get is going to be made(specially)to accomodate the larger motor,and headers/exhaust,if not,it could make all the changes counterproductive.<br>I've heard some bad things about the Jensen amps;like low performance per the advertised ratings,maybe someone else has more direct experience to reflect upon this one.
Z||000002||Formula||07-21-99||01:14 PM||||About the Jensen amp...<br>I had a a 400x1w amp bridged to 4 12" american bass subwoofers, (it sounded great, no distortion or anything, and they're only a hundred bucks). It sounded better then my friends precision power amp did on my subs. Strange but true. Also to note, It survived a totaled car accident(rice rocket), after being sold it had 2 power surges and is still pumping bass in a econobox escort somewhere around town.<br>I'm thinking that I was lucky and bought the cream of the crop amp.  (I'm a former rice rocket fan converted to american power example... civic to fiero formula) thanks for the tip about the cpu chip.
Z||000003||Eric||07-21-99||01:32 PM||||Sounds like you may come in under budget.  I don't plan to spend more than $1200 on my 3.1 rebuild and thats not even cheaping out on parts.  The exaust should cost you no more than $1000.  You can get coated headers for about $500 and a Borla cat back for another $500.  I can't comment about the paint job, but I've heard prices ranging from $1000 to $4000.  I want to replce my seats with a set from a later model car with leather.  They shouldn't be hard to find or too expensive.  
Z||000004||batboy||07-21-99||10:29 PM||||Your car will be awesome if you ever get all those things done. I can't wait to see a pic. A couple of comments: don't skimp on the head work, that's where you can make or lose lots of power. You didn't mention what cam you plan on using, but the GM hi-po cam or Crane CompuCam are both excellent choices that work well with computer controlled V6 cars.<p>Sorry Formula, but I'm with LowCG as far as the Jensen amps go. Jensen used to be really good, but nowadays they are a shadow of their former self. They are inexpensive, I will give them that and sound better than nothing. <br>I just got a Rockford-Fosgate Punch amp and 8 inch Punch sub and I'm extremely thrilled. They are a perfect match and sound excellent. Ok, the Punch amp is nearly twice the price, but it sounds three times as good as my Profile California Series amp in one of my other cars (the Profile has similar specs and price as the Jensen).<p>Keep us posted on your progress and good luck.
Z||000005||Cooter||07-21-99||10:54 PM||||Batboy- aint you too old for all the thumping stereo stuff????? Huh? Did you say something? I cant hear anymore, too many subs in too small of an area. LOL!
Z||000006||batboy||07-22-99||06:01 AM||||Cooter, I didn't know that you could see my graying hair through the internet. Just because I'm an old fart don't mean I can't still enjoy listening to Pink Floyd while I'm cruising on a good sounding stereo that pumps out a little bass. With one 8 inch sub and one 140 watt amp, this would not be called a ghetto thumper. Eh, speak up sonny, I can't hardly hear ya!
Z||000007||BluByYou||07-22-99||06:44 AM||||Hey Batboy - tell me about your Fosgate amp/sub set-up.  I've looked at my car, ('87 GT), and short of putting my passenger seat completely forward, I haven't found a good place to put an adequately sized box.  I haven't considered cutting anything yet, and really don't want to if it can be helped.  What did you do ???
Z||000008||Formula||07-22-99||08:23 AM||||I do agree that rockford fosgate is a 10x better stereo company then Jensen.  But as far as prices go, I don't think I need a high quality amp for the sub. I will need a high qaulity amp if I get those boston acoustics speakers. As for the Cam I will most likely go for the GM hi-po. 
Z||000009||BluByYou||07-22-99||08:36 AM||||Formula:  If you're interested, I have Bouston Acoustics in the rear, Kickers in the dash and an Alpine deck.  It sounds good, but a little "bright".  I need low-end badly.  I also have the BA's in another car (4x6's, identical to the Fiero's) and they sound much better accompanied with bass.  They are great speakers for mid & highs - very precise.
Z||000010||GREENDANO||07-22-99||08:03 PM||||My two cents worth....I have an enclosure made by Patrick Malougney, spelling?  It has space for an 8" sub.  I bought a sub that had the right specs to fit the small box that fits in the stock Fiero sub location.  I don't know how to get ahold of him.  Maybe someone here does???  I have a garbage(cheap) Urban Audio amp that I got for $30.  I ran over budget buying the speaker.  I want a new amp, as I'm sure the sub/encloser will handle more power. Maybe Christmas from the Wife..? It hits very well for a single DVC 8" sub.  I end up turning it down all the time, I have a home-made, simple, sub cross-over that I built with the control mounted in the LH ashtray.  Got the plan from a Radio Shack book.  It works very well.<br>  Motor....$1200 is low for the budget, unless you get a junkyard motor that just needs gaskets.  I spent $600 just in machine work to the block and heads.  It was a 142,000 mile motor,BTW.  BATBOY is right about the heads.  Just get a good 5 angle valve job like the stockers.  A three angle job is also just as cost effective for a street motor.  I have the GM performance cam, with 0.427 and 0.454 lift.  It pulls strong, but, I lost about 25 miles range per tank-full.  This cam is in a 0.040" over motor with a Camaro 3.1 crank, the total displacement is 3.2 liters.
Z||000011||batboy||07-22-99||09:30 PM||||BluByYou, there was a factory subwoofer option for Fieros. They mounted a 6 inch sub under the dash with a small amp under the console. Look up under the passenger side dash and you'll see a empty space. I've heard of people putting an 8 incher up in there, but I decided that look like too much work.<p>What I did was mount a sealed sub box behind the passenger seat and an amp behind the driver seat. The amp is only about 2-1/2 inches tall, so it don't take up much room. There are a couple of options for getting a box to fit behind the passenger seat without having to slide the seat too far forward (you do have to go up a few notches).<p>Either custom build a box or have a stereo shop build one for you. If you build one yourself, research how to do it, it's not as easy as it sounds. If you have a custom one built, use a wedge shape and put the narrow end down and the wide end up. Also mount it to the firewall about halfway up from the floor with some "L" brackets, this will give you more room, but you (I mean your passengers) can't recline the seat very far back, but it still gives reasonable leg room for all but the very tallest people. It's also not real obvious from the outside prying eyes, especially if the box carpet (covering) sort of matches your interior color. <p>The other option is to find a pre-made box that will fit. I found a box at a local stereo shop that was 12" X 12" X 5" and that worked pretty good. These were made to fit under van or SUV seats I think. The Rockford-Fosgate 8 inch sub needs between 0.3 to 0.5 cubic feet of sealed box to work good and this box I found is about 0.4 cubic feet. Hope this answers your question.
Z||000012||lowCG||07-23-99||12:40 AM||||Here's the side dimensions for the underdash cabinet(outside dimensions):<br>      3"<br>     --|<br> 6".   ||  8-1/2"<br>  |____|<br>    3-1/2"<br>The cabinet is 11" wide,speaker offset to one side,mounted with a strap,screwed to the top(6" side)of the cabinet with four screws,and a raised-up section in the middle(of the strap) to get a nut onto the stud that goes through the strap.An additional bracket on the side will attach to the structure on the kick-panel,dash mount if believe.That bracket should be put onto the cabinet with three screws.I used 3/4" MDF,no ports.Oh,speaker faces downward. 
Z||000013||Formula||07-23-99||08:30 AM||||Has anyone thought about a free-air sub under the dash?  One of my friends had one in his trunk but I think it has to be in the trunk. 
Z||000014||BluByYou||07-23-99||09:01 AM||||I never considered it - can you get any more info on it?  I've never listened to an "open air" sub, but I'd like to know more about them.
Z||000015||Formula||07-23-99||09:42 AM||||I think cerwin-vega makes them. I'll look for info in a little while.
Z||000016||lowCG||07-23-99||01:14 PM||||OOpS!<br>Ok ,picture a box,with 8-1/2" X 7" X 11" wide<p>The side dimension of 8-1/2" X 7" is a rectangle,won't fit into the car,so,going from opposite corners of that end plate,go towards one corner 3",and 3-1/2",from the opposite corner,and make a mark.You then cut off this triangle portion of the box,and cover the hole,completing the cabinet.<br>Hope this makes sense to anyone interested.
